3203125, -0.034027099609375, -0.0010309219360351562, 0.004238128662109375, 0.00220489501953125, -0.02410888671875, -0.0123138427734375, -0.01287078857421875, -0.0029811859130859375, -0.03375244140625, 0.0006947517395019531, 0.036224365234375, -0.01505279541015625, 0.0243377685546875, -0.007556915283203125, 0.0556640625, 0.0033206939697265625, -0.0299224853515625, 0.012359619140625, -0.056549072265625, 0.025360107421875, -0.0020999908447265625, -0.014862060546875, 0.00799560546875, -0.00415802001953125, -0.003925323486328125, -0.014678955078125, 0.024017333984375, -0.00870513916015625, 0.01126861572265625, 0.009490966796875, -0.0273590087890625, -0.07281494140625, 0.01824951171875, 0.01100921630859375, 0.0181121826171875, -0.038330078125, 0.0237884521484375, -0.05010986328125, 0.01512908935546875, -0.0287322998046875, -0.00290679931640625, -0.0270233154296875, -0.02227783203125, 0.008148193359375, 0.0133819580078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Black holes are among the most fascinating and extreme objects studied in astronomy and astrophysics. A black hole is an astronomical body so compact that its gravity prevents anything, including light, from escaping. In the framework of Albert Einstein's theory of general relativity\u2014which describes gravitation as the bending or curvature of spacetime caused by mass and energy\u2014if a mass is compressed within a certain critical radius, it will form a black hole.\n\nThis critical radius is known as the Schwarzschild radius, and it specifically applies to non-rotating, uncharged black holes (called Schwarzschild black holes). Other types of black holes include rotating (Kerr) black holes, which possess angular momentum, and charged (Reissner-Nordstr\u00f6m) black holes, which carry electric charge. Schwarzschild black holes are spherically symmetric and have no charge or spin, while Kerr black holes are characterized by their rotation, leading to phenomena such as frame dragging and the presence of an ergosphere. Reissner-Nordstr\u00f6m black holes, on the other hand, have electric charge, which alters the spacetime geometry and can result in multiple horizons. Observationally, these differences can affect the dynamics of matter and light near the black hole, such as the shape of the shadow or the behavior of accretion disks, though distinguishing these types directly remains challenging.\n\nThe boundary beyond which nothing can escape a black hole is called the event horizon. Importantly, the event horizon is not a locally detectable surface but a global feature of spacetime: it marks the point of no return, beyond which events cannot influence an outside observer. For an object falling into a black hole, crossing the event horizon produces no noticeable change locally; the object would not detect anything unusual at that precise moment. However, to a distant observer, the object appears to slow down and never quite crosses the horizon. The strength of tidal forces\u2014differences in gravitational pull\u2014near the event horizon depends on the black hole's mass, scaling inversely with mass. For example, at the event horizon of a stellar-mass black hole (about 10 times the mass of the Sun), tidal forces can be strong enough to spaghettify an object, while for a supermassive black hole (millions of solar masses), the tidal forces at the horizon are much weaker and could be survivable for a human.\n\nTransitioning from classical to quantum aspects, general relativity predicts that every black hole should contain a central singularity, a point where the curvature of spacetime becomes infinite and the known laws of physics break down. However, this prediction is based on classical theory, and it is widely believed that quantum effects\u2014such as those described by candidate quantum gravity theories like loop quantum gravity or string theory\u2014could modify or resolve this singularity. These ideas remain theoretical speculation, as there is currently no consensus or experimental evidence for any particular quantum gravity theory.\n\nA key quantum prediction is Hawking radiation, a process by which black holes can emit thermal radiation due to quantum effects near the event horizon, as proposed by Stephen Hawking. This implies that black holes have a temperature and entropy, connecting black hole physics to thermodynamics. However, Hawking radiation has not yet been directly observed, primarily because the predicted temperature for stellar or larger black holes is extremely low, making the radiation exceedingly faint compared to other astrophysical sources. As a result, the evaporation of black holes via Hawking radiation remains a theoretical expectation rather than an established experimental fact.\n\nBlack holes serve as important laboratories for testing our understanding of gravity, quantum physics, and the fundamental laws of the universe. Observations of gravitational waves from black hole mergers provide strong, though indirect, evidence for their existence, while studies of accretion disks and the motion of stars near black holes further support their presence. More recently, the Event Horizon Telescope has produced direct images of the shadow cast by a supermassive black hole, offering visual evidence of the black hole's presence and properties, though not of the event horizon itself.\n\nIn summary, black holes remain at the frontier of physics and astronomy, raising open questions about the nature of singularities, the unification of quantum mechanics and gravity, and the ultimate fate of information falling into black holes. In particular, the black hole information paradox\u2014whether information that falls into a black hole is lost or preserved\u2014remains a subject of active debate. Ongoing and future observations, as well as advances in theoretical physics, promise to deepen our understanding of these enigmatic objects."}}